# netpol-agent-egress.yaml.template
#
# The per-tier DEFAULT-DENY egress NetworkPolicy for an agent pod (03 §9/§10), rendered by
# `provision_13_apply_egress_policies.sh` and applied by it. The three committed exemplars under
# examples/gitops-repo/ are the REFERENCE RENDER of this template, byte-for-byte, and
# local-dev/tests/egress-policy-render.py fails if they drift — edit this file, then regenerate them.
#
# SHAPE = PURE ALLOWLIST. `policyTypes: [Egress]` plus a podSelector makes egress default-deny for the
# selected pods; only what is listed is reachable. There is deliberately NO 0.0.0.0/0 rule: a broad
# allow-with-exceptions would make "arbitrary hosts are unreachable" false, and every destination
# below had to be argued for individually.
#
# WHAT IS *NOT* HERE, AND WHY THAT IS THE POINT. The cloud metadata server is absent from the base
# allowlist, so raw node credentials are unreachable by omission rather than by a deny rule that a
# later edit could reorder away. When Workload Identity genuinely needs the metadata server, the
# install path appends the NARROW, PORT-BOUND block below (WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_METADATA_BLOCK) — never
# a whole-host allow. See common.sh:render_wi_metadata_block for the exact IP↔port pairings, which
# are dataplane-specific and are the single easiest thing in this file to get wrong.
#
# LIMITATION (hostname precision): NetworkPolicy is L3/L4 and cannot express hostnames, so GitHub is
# allowlisted by published IP CIDRs, which drift. Refresh them from https://api.github.com/meta. The
# hostname-precise L7 egress proxy remains deferred hardening (08 §5).
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: ${NETPOL_NAME}
  namespace: ${AGENT_NAMESPACE}
  labels:
    kube-agents/tier: ${AGENT_TIER}
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: gitops
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      kube-agents/tier: ${AGENT_TIER}
  policyTypes:
    - Egress
  egress:
    # 1) DNS — cluster resolution. Without this, every name-based egress fails before it starts,
    #    including the ExternalName CNAME chain rule 2 depends on.
    - to:
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system
      ports:
        - protocol: UDP
          port: 53
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 53
    # 2) The cluster's shared agent services in ${CONTROL_NAMESPACE} — Inference (LiteLLM, :80, the
    #    ONLY inference path, per-tier virtual key) and the GitHub/Workload-Identity token broker
    #    (github-token-minter, :8080, short-lived scoped tokens; the agent holds no long-lived write
    #    credential). The controller renders both hostnames into the POD'S OWN namespace, so a
    #    developer-team agent reaches them through the ExternalName aliases in its namespace; this
    #    rule is what admits that hop. It is one-way into the cluster's control namespace and never
    #    toward another tenant.
    #
    #    Default-deny applies to same-namespace traffic too, so the platform and cluster-admin tiers
    #    need this rule just as much as a developer-team agent does — their pods happen to already be
    #    in ${CONTROL_NAMESPACE}, which does not exempt them.
    - to:
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: ${CONTROL_NAMESPACE}
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 80
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 8080
    # 3) Google cloud APIs via Private Google Access (restricted.googleapis.com). Stable, well-known
    #    range — reads only (the agent's cloud GSA is viewer-only; the write path is a GitOps PR).
    #    This ALSO carries the eventingress Pub/Sub SUBSCRIBE leg (04 §4): pubsub.googleapis.com
    #    resolves to the same restricted VIP, so draining PRE-CREATED subscriptions needs no new
    #    CIDR or port. Subscribe-only — eventingress never creates a topic/subscription or publishes.
    - to:
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: 199.36.153.8/30
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 443
    # 4) GitHub — the agent pushes suggestion/provisioning branches and opens PRs with a Minty-minted
    #    token. GitHub's four published, stable IPv4 blocks (api + git + web, api.github.com/meta).
    #    The per-region /32s in that feed are deliberately omitted: they churn weekly and an
    #    allowlist nobody refreshes is an allowlist that silently breaks.
    - to:
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: 192.30.252.0/22
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: 185.199.108.0/22
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: 140.82.112.0/20
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: 143.55.64.0/20
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 443
${EGRESS_OPTIONAL_BLOCKS}
